<h2>The Samar Ghosh framework</h2>
<p>In <em>Samar Ghosh</em>, a three-Judge Bench surveyed English and Indian authority and set out illustrative — expressly non-exhaustive — instances of mental cruelty. The ones that recur daily in family-court practice include:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sustained abusive and humiliating treatment</strong> calculated to torture or render the spouse&rsquo;s life miserable, including repeated taunts about family, income or appearance.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Prolonged denial of the marital relationship</strong> without cause, or unilateral decisions of lasting consequence — refusal to have a child, sterilisation without the spouse&rsquo;s knowledge or consent.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Indifference and neglect</strong> — a sustained attitude of coldness, financial strangulation, or abandonment of matrimonial obligations falling short of desertion.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Wounding allegations</strong> — baseless charges of unchastity, insanity or criminality, whether in conversation, letters, social circles or court pleadings.</div>
</div>

<h2>How the ground is actually proved</h2>
<p>Mental cruelty is almost always proved through the petitioner&rsquo;s own testimony corroborated by contemporaneous material. Delhi family courts look for:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Material</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Dated, particularised pleadings</td><td>Vague omnibus allegations (&ldquo;always insulted me&rdquo;) are discounted; specific incidents with dates, places and witnesses survive cross-examination</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Messages, emails, recordings</td><td>Contemporaneous abuse in writing is the strongest corroboration; authenticity and completeness are tested, so produce full threads, not excerpts</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Complaints and their outcomes</td><td>Police complaints, CAW Cell proceedings and their results cut both ways — a baseless complaint supports the cruelty plea of the person falsely accused</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical and psychological records</td><td>Treatment for stress or depression during the relevant period connects conduct to effect</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conduct during litigation</td><td>Scandalous, unproved allegations added in written statements can themselves constitute fresh cruelty</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The recurring fact-patterns in Delhi</h2>
<p>Three clusters dominate contested cruelty trials. First, the <strong>false-prosecution cluster</strong>: a Section 498A/406 FIR or CAW Cell complaint that collapses, followed by a cruelty petition resting on the ordeal of arrest, bail and trial. Courts examine whether the acquittal was on merits or on a technicality, and whether the allegations were inherently improbable. Second, the <strong>humiliation cluster</strong>: sustained degradation before family, colleagues or children, proved through witnesses and writings. Third, the <strong>withdrawal cluster</strong>: prolonged, unexplained denial of companionship and conjugal life, often coupled with living as strangers under one roof — a pattern that also intersects with constructive desertion.</p>

<h2>Defending a cruelty petition</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Contextualise, don&rsquo;t just deny.</strong> Conduct admitted but explained (provocation, mutual quarrels, the petitioner&rsquo;s own conduct) often blunts the cumulative-effect argument better than blanket denial.</p>
  <p><strong>Watch your written statement.</strong> Fresh defamatory allegations made recklessly in defence can hand the petitioner a new, easily proved instance of cruelty.</p>
  <p><strong>Condonation matters.</strong> Resumption of cohabitation with knowledge of the conduct can amount to condonation under Section 23(1)(b) HMA — though revival of cruelty revives the condoned conduct too.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer discusses the general law of mental cruelty for information. It is not legal advice; whether particular conduct amounts to cruelty is an intensely fact-specific question.</div>
